00:44:33.660 --> 00:45:00.930
<v Neil Drumming>Act Four, Childhood's End. Looking into Afrofuturism over the past few months, I found myself thinking about time and space a lot. Time, because so many of these stories involve people thinking about the past or the future, or seeing the past and the future in the here and now, and space because all of that time travel is cued by very specific places-- Detroit, or Mount Vernon, or Philadelphia, or wherever.
<v Neil Drumming>第四幕，童年的终结。在过去几个月里审视非洲未来主义，我发现自己一直在思考时间和空间。时间，因为这些故事中有许多涉及人们思考过去或未来，或者在此时此地看到过去和未来；空间，因为所有那些时间旅行都是由非常具体的地点触发的——底特律、弗农山庄、费城，或者其他地方。

00:45:00.930 --> 00:45:35.400
<v Neil Drumming>During those same months, I also became sort of obsessed with these two videos that I saw on YouTube. I couldn't help connecting them in my mind, because for me they were both about a particularly precious intersection of time and space. The time is that period when children have left school for the day, but haven't yet made it home. And the space is that distance, however many blocks, however many miles it takes those kids to walk from one to the other. In other words, these two videos are about those moments when kids are out in the world on their own and the adults they bump into.
<v Neil Drumming>在那些相同的月份里，我也变得有点痴迷于我在YouTube上看到的这两个视频。我无法不在脑海中将它们联系起来，因为对我来说，它们都是关于时间和空间一个特别珍贵的交集。时间是孩子们放学后但还没到家的一段时期。空间是那些孩子从一个地方走到另一个地方所花费的距离，无论多少街区，多少英里。换句话说，这两个视频是关于孩子们独自在世界各地以及他们偶然遇到的成年人。

00:45:35.400 --> 00:45:53.820
<v Neil Drumming>You may have seen the first video. It takes place in Atlantic City. Two black teenagers in sweatpants and sneakers are fighting in the middle of a residential street on their way home from school when a slightly older black Man, Ibn Ali Miller, emerges from camera right and puts an end to the fight with a stern but passionate talking to.
<v Neil Drumming>你可能看过第一个视频。它发生在大西洋城。两个穿着运动裤和运动鞋的黑人青少年在放学回家的路上在住宅街道中间打架，这时一个稍微年长点的黑人男人，伊本·阿里·米勒，从镜头右侧出现，用一番严厉但充满激情的话语结束了这场争斗。

00:45:53.820 --> 00:46:09.170
<v Neil Drumming>The clip went viral. Miller was honored by the city. Steve Harvey had him on his couch. I saw it for the first time on my Facebook feed this past winter, and I viewed it on YouTube many times since. It does not disappoint. It gets better every time I watch it.
<v Neil Drumming>视频疯传。米勒受到了城市的表彰。史蒂夫·哈维请他上了节目。去年冬天我第一次在Facebook上看到它，从那以后我在YouTube上看了很多次。它没让人失望。每次看都觉得更好。

00:46:09.170 --> 00:46:14.130
<v Neil Drumming>[KIDS TAUNTING, LAUGHING]
<v Neil Drumming>[孩子们嘲笑，大笑]

00:46:14.130 --> 00:46:24.030
<v Neil Drumming>When it begins, the two boys grapple and flail at each other sloppily-- one tall, one chubby, both defiant. Other kids crowd around, recording with their cell phones, egging them on.
<v Neil Drumming>当它开始时，两个男孩笨拙地抓扯着对方——一个高个子，一个胖乎乎，都充满敌意。其他孩子围在周围，用手机录制，煽风点火。

00:46:24.030 --> 00:46:28.560
<v Teenager>Square up. Go ahead, [BLEEP] off him. [BLEEP].
<v Teenager>对准。来啊，[哔]揍他。[哔]。

00:46:28.560 --> 00:46:38.310
<v Neil Drumming>Then, seemingly out of nowhere, Miller bops into frame, his hands in his pockets, wearing work boots and a knit cap. He immediately starts to call out the kids who are recording.
<v Neil Drumming>然后，看似毫无征兆，米勒跳入画面，双手插兜，戴着工作靴和针织帽。他立即开始喊那些正在录制的孩子们。

00:46:38.310 --> 00:46:47.760
<v Ibn Ali Miller>Everybody on your phones though. Oh, y'all, y'all the real cowards. Record that, too. Everybody on a phone, record that. Everybody record on the phone real cowards.
<v Ibn Ali Miller>不过大家都在用手机。噢，你们，你们是真正的懦夫。录下那个，也是。每个人都用手机，录下。每个人用手机录制真正的懦夫。

00:46:47.760 --> 00:46:51.810
<v Neil Drumming>Cowards, Miller calls them. Then he turns his attention to the two boys in the center.
<v Neil Drumming>懦夫，米勒叫他们。然后他转向中间的两个男孩。

00:46:51.810 --> 00:47:01.730
<v Ibn Ali Miller>Listen, they all laughing, little brother. But you really upset. And you really upset. You more mad than he is. I can see it in your face. So you fighting for a reason. He only fighting you because you want to fight him.
<v Ibn Ali Miller>听着，他们都在笑，小兄弟。但你真的很沮丧。你也真的很沮丧。你比他更愤怒。我从你脸上能看出来。所以你是为了理由而战。他只是因为你想打他才和你打。

00:47:01.730 --> 00:47:05.780
<v Teenager>No, he came to me. He touched me. I wasn't [BLEEP]. I don't even know.
<v Teenager>不，他先来找我。他碰了我。我没[哔]。我甚至不知道。

00:47:05.780 --> 00:47:07.290
<v Ibn Ali Miller>So you're defending yourself?
<v Ibn Ali Miller>所以你在自卫？

00:47:07.290 --> 00:47:20.050
<v Neil Drumming>Miller listens. And then he tells the boys something they probably hadn't considered, that they were most likely tricked into fighting by someone who thought it would be entertaining. And now all of their so-called friends are recording them go at it.
<v Neil Drumming>米勒听着。然后他告诉男孩们一些他们可能没考虑过的事情，他们很可能是被某人诱骗去打架，那个人觉得这很有趣。现在他们所有所谓的“朋友”都在录下他们打斗。

00:47:20.050 --> 00:47:26.470
<v Ibn Ali Miller>Look around. Who ill-advised you? You told you wrong? Who told you wrong? You want to answer that? I'm saying, who told you wrong, little bro?
<v Ibn Ali Miller>环顾四周。谁给你出了坏主意？谁告诉你错了？谁告诉你错了？你想回答吗？我说，谁告诉你错了，小兄弟？

00:47:26.470 --> 00:47:44.890
<v Ibn Ali Miller>And the only reason I'm saying this to all y'all, yo, because you're almost men. All of y'all, yo. You're almost men. Y'all ain't kids no more. Y'all girls ain't little girls no more. Look, yo. Y'all men, yo. Y'all men, yo. 14, 15, 16-- y'all men. Start acting like it, yo. We ain't gonna get nowhere like this, yo.
<v Ibn Ali Miller>我之所以对你们所有人说这个，是因为你们快要成为男人了。你们所有人。你们不再是小孩子了。你们女孩不再是小女孩了。看，你们是男人。你们是男人。14岁、15岁、16岁——你们是男人。开始表现得像个男人。我们这样下去什么也得不到。

00:47:44.890 --> 00:48:06.310
<v Neil Drumming>We ain't going to get nowhere like this, he says. Here, what Miller does that I love is steer the conversation away from personal grievances towards something bigger. He tries to tell these kids that they are part of a community. He tells them that their actions have consequences and that those consequences only get more serious as they get older. Without condescending to them, Miller talks to them about their futures.
<v Neil Drumming>我们这样下去什么也得不到，他说。米勒做得我喜欢的一点是，将谈话从个人恩怨引向更宏大的层面。他试图告诉这些孩子，他们是一个社区的一部分。他告诉他们，他们的行为有后果，而这些后果只会随着年龄增长变得更严重。米勒没有屈尊俯就，而是与他们谈论他们的未来。

00:48:06.310 --> 00:48:18.840
<v Ibn Ali Miller>You think it's a game out here? Ain't no game out here. It's real out here, little bro. Word up. Don't make your parents look like this, yo. If y'all live around here, y'all live somewhere good. Don't mess that up.
<v Ibn Ali Miller>你觉得外面是游戏吗？外面可不是游戏。外面是现实，小兄弟。听着。别让你的父母看起来像这样。如果你们住在这儿附近，你们住在一个不错的地方。别把这一切搞砸了。

00:48:18.840 --> 00:48:35.530
<v Neil Drumming>He's no longer just trying to get them to save face or avoid humiliation. He's warning the boys that as they become men, young black men, the world will become even more dangerous for them. Miller won't leave until the two shake hands. They don't want to at first. Only the chubby kid extends his hand.
<v Neil Drumming>他不再只是试图让他们保住面子或避免羞辱。他警告男孩们，当他们成为男人，年轻黑人男性时，世界对他们来说会变得更危险。米勒不离开直到两人握手。起初他们不想。只有胖乎乎的孩子伸出了手。

00:48:35.530 --> 00:48:39.910
<v Ibn Ali Miller>Y'all don't shake hands or I'm not leaving, bro. I'm not leaving, bro. Shake hands, bro.
<v Ibn Ali Miller>你们如果不握手我就不离开，兄弟。我不离开，兄弟。握手，兄弟。

00:48:39.910 --> 00:48:41.450
<v Teenager>Just shake his hand, bro.
<v Teenager>跟他握手，兄弟。

00:48:41.450 --> 00:48:44.910
<v Neil Drumming>The other kid doesn't. The chubby kid pulls his hand back.
<v Neil Drumming>另一个孩子没动。胖乎乎的孩子缩回了手。

00:48:44.910 --> 00:48:50.730
<v Ibn Ali Miller>Put your hand back out, bro. He gonna shake it this time, I promise you. Put your hand back out, bro. He gonna shake it, bro.
<v Ibn Ali Miller>把手伸出来，兄弟。我保证他这次会握手的。把手伸出来，兄弟。他会握手的，兄弟。

00:48:50.730 --> 00:48:59.400
<v Neil Drumming>He does.
<v Neil Drumming>他握了。

00:48:59.400 --> 00:49:17.010
<v Neil Drumming>It's actually difficult for me to express how moved I was by this video. The beauty of it is that even while Miller is insisting that these teenagers become responsible for themselves, his actions demonstrate that they're not necessarily alone, that there is compassion, and wisdom, and guidance available to them.
<v Neil Drumming>实际上我很难表达这个视频让我有多感动。它的美在于，即使米勒坚持要求这些青少年为自己负责，他的行为也证明他们不一定孤单，还有同情心、智慧和指导提供给他们。

00:49:17.010 --> 00:49:32.190
<v Neil Drumming>It was like a window into a world where people who don't know each other still give a damn, a window that I could rewind, and re-watch, and forward to my friends to click Like. It was at once exhilarating and comforting. I rode that high for a week, maybe two.
<v Neil Drumming>这就像一扇窗户，看到一个人们即使不认识彼此也会互相关心的世界，一扇我可以倒带、重看并转发给朋友点赞的窗户。它既让人振奋又让人感到安慰。我那股兴奋劲持续了一周，也许两周。

00:49:32.190 --> 00:49:59.010
<v Neil Drumming>Then another video popped up in my Facebook feed. This one is shakier and from a low angle, obviously recorded surreptitiously. The view is of the intersection of Bedford Avenue and Campus Road, Midwood High School on one side of the street. Brooklyn College is on the other. I recognized it immediately because I used to live 10 minutes away. There's still snow on the ground, and the cell phone is buffeted about by wind.
<v Neil Drumming>然后另一个视频出现在我的Facebook动态里。这个视频摇晃得厉害，从低角度拍摄，显然是偷偷录制的。视图是贝德福德大道和校园路的交汇处，一边是米德伍德高中。另一边是布鲁克林学院。我立即认了出来，因为我曾经住在10分钟路程外。地上还有积雪，手机被风吹得晃动。

00:49:59.010 --> 00:50:16.370
<v Neil Drumming>Its camera wobbles up behind two uniformed police officers walking slowly down Campus Road. The one on the left has both hands in his pockets. The one on the right has his baton already drawn. A handful of black teenagers walk obstinately before them, headed away toward Flatbush Avenue.
<v Neil Drumming>镜头摇摇晃晃地跟在两个缓慢沿着校园路行走的穿制服警察身后。左边的那个双手插在口袋里。右边的那个警棍已经拔出来了。几个黑人青少年在他们前面固执地走着，朝弗拉特布什大道走去。

00:50:16.370 --> 00:50:30.320
<v Neil Drumming>There's no way to know for sure what happened before the video started rolling. There were, most likely, the beginnings of a snowball fight, but it's unclear who was unhappy about that. The police were not called. They were already on site.
<v Neil Drumming>无法确切知道视频开始前发生了什么。很有可能，是一场打雪仗的开始，但不清楚是谁对此不满。警察没有被叫来。他们就在现场。

00:50:30.320 --> 00:50:43.700
<v Neil Drumming>In the footage, one of the cops says, come on, let's go, though the kids are already going. The cop on the right reaches out. It looks like he shoves a boy in a pink hoodie. The boy flinches, snapping his head around.
<v Neil Drumming>在画面中，一名警察说，来吧，我们走吧，尽管孩子们已经在走了。右边的警察伸出手。看起来他推了一个穿着粉色连帽衫的男孩。男孩畏缩了一下，猛地转过头。

00:50:43.700 --> 00:50:55.580
<v Neil Drumming>Stop touching me, he says. The cop responds with, what are you going to do about it, which is obnoxious and a stupid question. But that doesn't stop him from asking it at least five more times, like a kid taunting another kid.
<v Neil Drumming>别碰我，他说。警察回答，你想拿它怎么办，这既讨厌又是个愚蠢的问题。但这并没有阻止他至少再问五次，就像一个孩子在挑衅另一个孩子。

00:50:55.580 --> 00:51:02.360
<v Cop>What are you going to do about it? What are you going to do about it? What are you going to do about it? What are you going to do about it? What are you going to do about it?
<v Cop>你想拿它怎么办？你想拿它怎么办？你想拿它怎么办？你想拿它怎么办？你想拿它怎么办？

00:51:02.360 --> 00:51:23.780
<v Neil Drumming>At one point, one of the teenagers fires back, that's why we out. It sounds bad ass. But if you think about it, the kid is saying, we're walking away because we can't do anything about it. And we all know that. Another kid in the procession kneels down to pick up some snow. With his left hand, the cop on the right pulls his taser out of his holster and barks--
<v Neil Drumming>在一个时候，一个青少年回击道，这就是为什么我们要走。听起来很酷。但如果你想想，那个孩子在说，我们走开是因为我们什么也做不了。我们都知道这一点。行列里的另一个孩子弯下腰去捡雪。右边的警察左手掏出泰瑟枪，咆哮道——

00:51:23.780 --> 00:51:27.590
<v Cop>Pick that [BLEEP] up again. Pick that [BLEEP] up again.
<v Cop>再捡那个[哔]东西试试。再捡那个[哔]东西试试。

00:51:27.590 --> 00:51:39.200
<v Neil Drumming>Pick that [BLEEP] up again. Rock, paper, scissors, snowball, taser. The kids keep walking away. The cop yells, pick that [BLEEP] up again one more time, and follows with--
<v Neil Drumming>再捡那个[哔]东西试试。石头、剪子、布、雪球、泰瑟枪。孩子们继续走开。警察又吼了一次“再捡那个[哔]东西试试”，并接着说——

00:51:39.200 --> 00:51:41.180
<v Cop>Do you want to ride the lightning?
<v Cop>你想体验闪电骑行吗？

00:51:41.180 --> 00:51:59.490
<v Neil Drumming>Do you want to ride the lightning? The video ends as the kids cross the street to safety, and the cops stop on the corner. I know. I know it seems like you can see worse, more unjust things happen to black people on YouTube just about any day of the week. Here, fortunately, no one was hurt.
<v Neil Drumming>你想体验闪电骑行吗？视频结束时，孩子们穿过街道到达安全地带，警察停在拐角处。我知道。我知道这看起来好像你几乎每天都能在YouTube上看到更糟糕、更不公正的事情发生在黑人身上。在这里，幸运的是，没人受伤。

00:51:59.490 --> 00:52:30.890
<v Neil Drumming>But this video was still hard to watch, especially having seen how Ibn Ali Miller talked those kids down in Atlantic City. Whereas Miller was trying to get the teenagers to think a little bit more like adults, the cop in this video sounded like a teenager himself, bullying and provoking. What's worse, he almost seemed to be enjoying it. It's hard to imagine he cared anything at all about what happened to those kids after they deserted that corner.
<v Neil Drumming>但这个视频依然很难看，尤其是看过伊本·阿里·米勒在大西洋城是怎么劝说那些孩子之后。米勒试图让青少年多像成年人一样思考，而视频里的警察听起来就像他自己是个青少年，霸凌和挑衅。更糟的是，他似乎很享受。很难想象他一点也不在乎那些孩子在离开那个拐角后发生了什么。

00:52:30.890 --> 00:52:43.310
<v Neil Drumming>I told my friends about this video too. I sent the link around. It wasn't as popular a clip as the one with Miller. I read everything I could about it. I found out that the kids most likely went to Midwood High School.
<v Neil Drumming>我也告诉了朋友关于这个视频的事。我发了链接。它不如米勒那个视频受欢迎。我阅读了所有能找到的关于它的内容。我发现孩子们很可能是去米德伍德高中的。

00:52:43.310 --> 00:53:00.050
<v Neil Drumming>And a few weeks later, I went to Midwood High School and stood on the corner of Bedford Avenue and Campus Road. I wanted to find someone who had witnessed the incident. I was angry on behalf of those young men, and I wanted to know how they felt. I found one of them immediately, and then the others found me.
<v Neil Drumming>几周后，我去了米德伍德高中，站在贝德福德大道和校园路的拐角处。我想找一个目击了事件的人。我为那些年轻人感到愤怒，我想知道他们的感受。我立即找到了其中一个，然后其他人也找到了我。

00:53:00.050 --> 00:53:01.370
<v Neil Drumming>Wait, which one were you?
<v Neil Drumming>等等，你是哪一个？

00:53:01.370 --> 00:53:02.840
<v Teenager>Me? I was the Black kid.
<v Teenager>我？我是那个黑人孩子。

00:53:02.840 --> 00:53:04.670
<v Neil Drumming>Well, they were all Black kids.
<v Neil Drumming>好吧，他们都是黑人孩子。

00:53:04.670 --> 00:53:06.560
<v Teenager>I was the blackest one, the tallest one.
<v Teenager>我是最黑的那个，最高的那个。

00:53:06.560 --> 00:53:28.940
<v Neil Drumming>They're in tapered gray sweats and colorful knapsacks. They told me they came to this spot almost every day, bought chicken, and fries, and rice, slap boxed with each other, kicked it with girls, and waited for the rest of their crew to go play basketball down the block. They wanted to know what my microphone was for and if they could get money and girls from being on the radio, and if I could buy them lunch.
<v Neil Drumming>他们穿着紧身灰色运动裤，背着彩色背包。他们告诉我他们几乎每天都来这个地方，买炸鸡、薯条和米饭，互相打闹，和女孩们在一起，等待他们的队友去街区篮球场玩。他们想知道我的麦克风是干什么用的，如果是上广播能不能拿到钱和女孩，还有我能不能给他们买午餐。

00:53:28.940 --> 00:53:49.430
<v Neil Drumming>I asked them what they were doing on the day that the video was recorded. One of them said he was cutting class. Others said they weren't. I asked them why they think the cops came, since their high school has its own security. One of them said that some other kids across the street were throwing snow and the cops thought it was us. One of the boys said it was them, but still, it was only snowballs.
<v Neil Drumming>我问他们视频录制那天在干什么。其中一个说他在逃课。其他人说没有。我问他们为什么认为警察会来，既然他们的高中有自己的保安。其中一个说对面的一些孩子在扔雪球，警察以为是我们。其中一个男孩说是他们，但无论如何，那只是雪球。

00:53:49.430 --> 00:53:50.840
<v Teenager 1>We were just being kids.
<v Teenager 1>我们只是像个孩子。

00:53:50.840 --> 00:53:52.200
<v Teenager 2>Yeah, we was being kids, chilling.
<v Teenager 2>是的，我们像个孩子，在那儿闲逛。

00:53:52.200 --> 00:53:54.520
<v Teenager 3>Yeah, and they were just wilding out.
<v Teenager 3>是的，他们只是在发疯。

00:53:54.520 --> 00:53:57.140
<v Teenager 1>We was just having snowballs-- we was having a snowball fight.
<v Teenager 1>我们只是在打雪仗。

00:53:57.140 --> 00:53:59.810
<v Teenager 3>Yeah, we were doing what we was doing. And we were just having fun.
<v Teenager 3>是的，我们在做我们做的事。我们只是玩得很开心。

00:53:59.810 --> 00:54:13.730
<v Neil Drumming>I asked if they were scared when it happened. A couple of 16-year-olds cooler than I'd ever been at that age said, no, not really. A 14-year-old said emphatically, of course. He was 6'2, my height.
<v Neil Drumming>我问他们发生那件事时害怕吗。几个比我在那个年纪酷得多的16岁孩子说，不，没怎么怕。一个14岁的孩子强调说，当然了。他6英尺2英寸，我的身高。

00:54:13.730 --> 00:54:34.980
<v Neil Drumming>I was surprised at how tall some of the boys were, none of them older than 16. I was also surprised at how not angry they seemed. I grew up about 45 minutes from here in a similar neighborhood in Queens, not so long ago, I thought. But when I asked them why they thought this had happened to them at all, one of the older boys talked to me like he was explaining why humans need food. The cop was white.
<v Neil Drumming>我对一些男孩的身高感到惊讶，没人超过16岁。我也对他们似乎一点都不生气感到惊讶。我小时候离这儿大约45分钟路程，在皇后区一个类似的社区，没多久前，我想。但当我问他们为什么认为这会发生在他们身上时，其中一个大一点的男孩谈论我，就像在解释人类为什么需要食物。那个警察是白人。

00:54:34.980 --> 00:54:39.690
<v Teenager 1>You know, they don't like Black people. And I thought-- I was like, oh, we about to-- [IMITATES TASER]
<v Teenager 1>你知道，他们不喜欢黑人。我想——我当时想，噢，我们要——[模仿泰瑟枪声]

00:54:39.690 --> 00:54:41.220
<v Teenager 2>Yeah, we about to get tased.
<v Teenager 2>是的，我们要被电了。

00:54:41.220 --> 00:54:55.950
<v Neil Drumming>Maybe this is just me, but I look back on walks home from school as my first real flirtations with independence, a sacred time and space between my teachers' rules and my parents' restrictions. The kids in that video, this was their school. This was their neighborhood.
<v Neil Drumming>也许这只是我，但回望放学回家的路，那是我第一次真正感受到独立的诱惑，那是我的老师规则和我父母限制之间一段神圣的时间和空间。那个视频里的孩子们，这是他们的学校。这是他们的社区。

00:54:55.950 --> 00:55:18.210
<v Neil Drumming>Most of the ones I talked to live only four or five blocks away. As far as they were concerned, this was their corner. I can't imagine what it would feel like to be firmly in your time and space and run up against a cop with a taser, threatening to electrocute you if you don't put down that snowball.
<v Neil Drumming>我交谈的大多数人只住在四五个街区外。对他们而言，这是他们的角落。无法想象身为你在自己的时间和空间中，遇到一个拿着泰瑟枪的警察，威胁如果你不放下那个雪球就要电击你，会是什么感觉。

00:55:18.210 --> 00:55:41.730
<v Neil Drumming>What's crazier to me is that I've walked through this intersection at Bedford Avenue and Campus Road, past the high school, and through the Brooklyn College campus many times in the last few years. Beneath its landmark gilded clock tower, Brooklyn College could be a campus quad from a corny 1950s film, except teeming with students from cultures all over New York. Something about it practically screams welcome, things only get better from here.
<v Neil Drumming>更让我疯狂的是，过去几年我多次走过贝德福德大道和校园路的这个路口，经过高中，穿过布鲁克林学院校园。在标志性的镀金钟楼下，布鲁克林学院可以是从一部老掉牙的1950年代电影中的校园广场，只是挤满了来自纽约各地文化的学生。它几乎在大声喊着欢迎，事情只会从这里变得更好。

00:55:41.730 --> 00:55:59.850
<v Neil Drumming>The young Black men in that video came out of their high school that day and stood at the edge of that promise. What could be the next great space and time in their lives was a college literally across the street, but they were shooed away, told they didn't belong. As far as metaphors go, this one couldn't be less ambiguous.
<v Neil Drumming>视频里的年轻黑人男性那天走出高中，站在承诺的边缘。他们生命中下一个伟大的时间和空间可能就是街对面的学院，但他们被赶走了，被告知他们不属于这里。就隐喻而言，这一个再明显不过了。

00:55:59.850 --> 00:56:14.290
<v Neil Drumming>But none of this seemed to bother the boys, or at least not that they let on. This was not the first time that they had been made to feel unwelcome so close to home and school, yet they weren't festering with resentment. They weren't seeing the tragedy in it that I was seeing.
<v Neil Drumming>但这些都没怎么困扰男孩们，至少他们没表现出来。这不是他们第一次在离家和学校这么近的地方感到不受欢迎，但他们并没有因为怨恨而腐烂。他们没有看到我所看到的悲剧。

00:56:14.290 --> 00:56:36.490
<v Neil Drumming>After I finished talking to them, I went across the street to find the Brooklyn College professor who'd recorded the incident. Alex Vitale teaches sociology, and also happens to run the school's policing and social justice project. Leaving work that day, he'd seen the two cops descend on Bedford Avenue and Campus Road, followed them. And it was his recording that ended up on YouTube, as well as a few local news websites.
<v Neil Drumming>在和他们谈完后，我过马路去找录制这段视频的布鲁克林学院教授。亚历克斯·维塔莱教授社会学，也碰巧运营学校的警务和社会正义项目。那天离开工作岗位，他看到那两名警察冲向贝德福德大道和校园路，就跟了过去。正是他的录像最终出现在YouTube以及一些当地新闻网站上。

00:56:36.490 --> 00:57:01.150
<v Neil Drumming>We talked briefly about how repeated incidents like this could affect the boys' psyches. He agreed that no matter how cool they seem now, over time their hopes for their own futures could possibly darken. But then Alex asked me how old I was. When I told him 43, he said that I had missed the height of the stop and frisk era. I was not a teenager or even a young man in New York City when unprompted police harassment was at its worst.
<v Neil Drumming>我们简单谈论了像这样反复发生的事件可能如何影响男孩们的心理。他同意无论他们现在看起来多酷，随着时间推移，他们对自己未来的希望可能会暗淡。但亚历克斯问我多大。当我告诉他43岁时，他说我错过了拦截盘查时代的高峰。当无缘无故的警察骚扰最严重时，我并不是纽约市的青少年或年轻人。

00:57:01.150 --> 00:57:16.810
<v Neil Drumming>So the taser incident outraged me, but what these kids had seen their older brothers, sisters, and in some cases parents go through had conditioned them to be a bit more stoic. It stung a little bit to have this white academic tell me that I couldn't quite relate to a young black man's experience.
<v Neil Drumming>所以泰瑟枪事件让我愤怒，但这些孩子看到他们的大哥哥大姐姐，在某些情况下是父母经历过的事情，已经让他们变得更坚忍了。被这个白人学者告知我无法完全体会年轻黑人男性的经历，确实有点刺痛。

00:57:16.810 --> 00:58:01.690
<v Neil Drumming>I guess when I first approached those boys, a part of me hoped that, like Ibn Ali Miller in the Atlantic City video, I would just roll up out of nowhere and know everything about their world that they know, plus some more. Instead, I was just another adult, taking their time and invading their space.
<v Neil Drumming>我想当我第一次接近那些男孩时，我内心一部分希望像大西洋城视频里的伊本·阿里·米勒一样，我能莫名其妙地出现，知道他们世界的一切，外加更多。相反，我只是另一个成年人，占用他们的时间并侵入他们的空间。

00:58:01.690 --> 00:58:22.030
<v Ira Glass>Our program was produced today by Neil Drumming and Stephanie Foo. Our staff includes Susan Burton, Ben Calhoun, Zoe Chace, Dina Chivvis, Sean Cole, Whitney Dangerfield, Kimberly Henderson, Chana Joffe-Walt, David Kestenbaum, Seth Lind, BA Parker, Robyn Semien, Christopher Swetala, Matt Tierney, Julie Whitaker, and Diane Wu. Our senior producer for today's show is Brian Reed. Additional help for this rerun from Noor Gill and Stowe Nelson.
<v Ira Glass>我们的节目今天由尼尔·德拉明和斯蒂芬妮·富制作。我们的员工包括苏珊·伯顿、本·卡尔霍恩、佐伊·蔡斯、迪娜·奇维斯、肖恩·科尔、惠特尼·丹格菲尔德、金伯利·亨德森、查娜·乔菲-沃尔特、大卫·克斯滕鲍姆、塞思·林德、BA·帕克、罗宾·塞米恩、克里斯托弗·斯韦塔拉、马特·蒂尔尼、朱莉·惠特克和黛安·吴。今天节目的高级制作人是布莱恩·里德。这次重播的其他帮助来自努尔·吉尔和斯托·尼尔森。

00:58:22.030 --> 00:58:44.470
<v Ira Glass>Special thanks today to Frank Nieto, Nalo Hopkinson, Detroit Robinson, Shani Hilton, Nailah Middleton, Steve Kaplan, Stephen Henderson, Lee DeVito, Solange Franklin, Evan Narcisse, Bryce Detroit, Xiomara, and Denmark Vessey. Our website, with this music video to go with the CLPPNG underwater sci-fi song you heard earlier-- also the artist Paul Davey did a piece of original art for today's show-- that's thisamericanlife.org.
<v Ira Glass>今天特别感谢弗兰克·涅托、纳洛·霍普金森、底特律·罗宾逊、沙尼·希尔顿、奈拉·米德尔顿、史蒂夫·卡普兰、斯蒂芬·亨德森、李·德维托、索兰格·富兰克林、埃文·纳西斯、布莱斯·底特律、西奥玛拉和丹麦·维西。我们的网站，有配合您刚才听到的CLPPNG水下科幻歌曲的音乐视频——还有艺术家保罗·戴维为今天的节目创作了一件原创艺术品——那是thisamericanlife.org。

00:58:44.470 --> 00:58:59.380
<v Ira Glass>This American Life is delivered to public radio stations by PRX, the Public Radio Exchange. Thanks, as always, to our program's co-founder, Mr. Torey Malatia, who had the most disappointing latte of his entire life this week. He asked for extra foam, extra cream, but it did not come that way.
<v Ira Glass>《美国生活》由公共广播交流电台PRX交付给公共广播电台。像往常一样感谢我们节目的联合创始人托雷·马拉蒂亚先生，他本周喝了他一生中最令人失望的拿铁。他要求多加泡沫，多加奶油，但它并没有那样送来。

00:58:59.380 --> 00:59:05.380
<v Ingrid Lafleur>It was, like, 110% black. It was super black. It was just black.
<v Ingrid Lafleur>它就像是110%的黑。它超级黑。它就是黑。

00:59:05.380 --> 00:59:10.380
<v Ira Glass>I'm Ira Glass, back next week with more stories of This American Life.
<v Ira Glass>我是艾拉·格拉斯，下周回来带来更多《美国生活》的故事。
